@@ -15,6 +15,12 @@ One entry per significant change: date, what changed, what was learned, what is
 - **Dev dependency added.** `flake8` in `requirements-devel.in`; `requirements-devel.txt` regenerated with `pip-compile` without `--upgrade` (only additions: flake8, mccabe, pycodestyle, pyflakes).
 - **Secret in tracked code, not changed.** `settings/common.py:249` holds a hard-coded `SECRET_KEY`. See the TODO in `AGENTS.md` and the `blocked` list of the harness build.
 
+## 2026-11-02: password recovery tokens expire
+
+- **What changed.** New setting `PASSWORD_RECOVERY_TOKEN_LIFETIME` (default `timedelta(hours=1)`) in `settings/common.py`. New nullable field `User.token_expires_at` (migration `users/0034_user_token_expires_at`). `password_recovery` sets a new token and `token_expires_at = timezone.now() + lifetime`. `change_password_from_recovery` accepts the token only if `token_expires_at` is in the future; expired tokens and legacy tokens without an expiration date get the same 400 "Token is invalid" as an unknown token, and the user does not change. On success, `change_password`, `change_email` and `User.cancel()` clear both the token and its expiration date.
+- **What we learned.** Tests that create a recovery token by hand (`tests/integration/test_users.py`, `tests/integration/resources_permissions/test_users_resources.py`) must now also set `token_expires_at` in the future. `make test` calls `make db-up`, which fails when another worktree's compose PostgreSQL already holds port 5432. The tests still work against that container, but its `test_taiga` database may be used by another session at the same time. To avoid this, the full suite was run with a settings module that sets a different test database name.
+- **What is left.** Existing tokens in real databases have no expiration date, so they stop working after the migration. Users must request a new recovery email. `docker/config.py` does not expose the new setting as an environment variable.
+
 ## 2026-11-01: fast gate output shows the test run
 
 - **What changed.** `make test-unit` (used by `make check-fast`) ran `pytest tests/unit --reuse-db -q`. The `-q` flag hides the pytest header ("test session starts", "collected N items") and the file names, so the harness verifier reported that the fast gate ran no tests, although 238 tests passed. The target now runs `pytest tests/unit --reuse-db -rA --tb=short`: the output shows the header, one line per test file, one `PASSED <test id>` line per test and the final summary (`238 passed`). No test or check was removed.
